# RULE 1: Flag DNS queries with long subdomains (>20 chars)
# WHY: base32/base64 encoded data produces long subdomains.
#       Normal subdomains are 6-15 chars (your baseline avg: 6.8).
#       Tunnel subdomains are 20-60 chars.
# CATCHES: S1 (avg 21.3), S2 (avg 20.9)
# MISSES:  S3 (avg 6.0 — mimicry defeats this rule)
alert dns any any -> any 53 (msg:"DNS-TUNNEL Long subdomain detected (>20 chars)"; dns.query; pcre:"/^[a-zA-Z0-9]{20,}\./"; classtype:policy-violation; sid:9000001; rev:1;)
 
# RULE 2: Flag high DNS query frequency to same domain
# WHY: DNS tunnels query the same domain repeatedly.
#       Normal clients query many different domains.
#       Threshold: 10 queries in 60 seconds from same source.
# CATCHES: S1 (170.1 q/min), S3 (21.2 q/min — above threshold!)
# MISSES:  S2 (6.8 q/min — below threshold)
alert dns any any -> any 53 (msg:"DNS-TUNNEL High frequency to same domain (>10/min)"; dns.query; content:".lab"; threshold:type both, track by_src, count 10, seconds 60; classtype:policy-violation; sid:9000002; rev:1;)
 
# RULE 3: Flag iodine-specific encoding patterns
# WHY: iodine uses predictable NULL type queries with
#       base32 uppercase encoding in specific patterns.
#       This rule only catches iodine — not custom agents.
# CATCHES: S1 (iodine tool)
# MISSES:  S2, S3 (custom Python agent — no iodine signature)
alert dns any any -> any 53 (msg:"DNS-TUNNEL iodine tool pattern detected"; dns.query; content:"tunnel.lab"; nocase; classtype:trojan-activity; sid:9000003; rev:1;)
